{%- comment -%}
  Reference ("was") pricing under the Omnibus Directive (EU) 2019/2161.

  The rule: when a price reduction is announced, the reference price shown must
  be the LOWEST price the trader applied during at least the 30 days before the
  reduction. Shopify's native `compare_at_price` is not that number — it is a
  free-text field the merchant types, and using it directly to display a "was"
  price is how stores end up making an unlawful price claim without noticing.

  So this snippet deliberately does NOT read compare_at_price. It reads a
  metafield that operations is responsible for maintaining from real price
  history, and it renders nothing at all when that metafield is absent.

  Failing closed is the point: no reference price is lawful; a wrong one is not.

  Source: research/eu-compliance-constraints.md §6.4.
  REQUIRES PROFESSIONAL VERIFICATION before launch.

  Accepts: product
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign lowest_30 = product.metafields.pricing.lowest_price_30d.value
-%}

{%- if lowest_30 and lowest_30 > variant.price -%}
  <p class="text-s text-muted">
    <s>{{ lowest_30 | money }}</s>
    <span>{{ 'product.price.lowest_30d' | t }}</span>
  </p>
{%- endif -%}
